    {
      "id": "math.radical-rules",
      "name": "Multiplying and dividing square roots",
      "requires": [
        "math.square-roots"
      ],
      "explain": {
        "en": {
          "text": "For positive a and b, √a × √b = √(ab) and √a ÷ √b = √(a/b). So √2 × √3 = √6. A product of two irrational numbers may be rational: √2 × √2 = 2. Also a√b = √(a²b). So 3√2 = √18, and √50 = 5√2."
        },
        "am": {
          "text": "a እና b አዎንታዊ ሲሆኑ √a × √b = √(ab) እና √a ÷ √b = √(a/b) ነው። ስለዚህ √2 × √3 = √6። የሁለት ኢ-ምክንያታዊ ቁጥሮች ብዜት ምክንያታዊ ሊሆን ይችላል፤ √2 × √2 = 2። ደግሞ a√b = √(a²b) ነው። ስለዚህ 3√2 = √18 እና √50 = 5√2።",
          "machine": true
        }
      },
      "screens": [
        {
          "kind": "guess",
          "step": "hook",
          "title": "Irrational times irrational",
          "text": "√2 is irrational. √3 and √8 are irrational too.",
          "ask": "Is the product of two irrational numbers always irrational? Guess.",
          "opts": [
            "Yes, always",
            "No, sometimes it is rational",
            "It is never irrational"
          ],
          "after": "Try √2 × √2 and √2 × √8 on your calculator. Then read on.",
          "ref": "Activity 1.11"
        },
        {
          "kind": "read",
          "step": "read",
          "title": "Activity 1.11: what happens?",
          "paras": [
            "1 + √2 is irrational. A rational number plus an irrational number is irrational.",
            "√2 × √3 = √6 is irrational.",
            "But √2 × √2 = 2 and √2 × √8 = 4 are rational.",
            "So a product of two irrational numbers is not always irrational."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "read",
          "step": "read",
          "title": "The product rule",
          "paras": [
            "If a > 0 and b > 0, then √a × √b = √(ab).",
            "Example 1. (a) √2 × √3 = √6.",
            "(b) (1/√3) × (2/√3) = 2/(√3 × √3) = 2/3.",
            "(c) Use the distributive law: (2 + √2)(1 + √5) = 2 + 2√5 + √2 + √10.",
            "(d) (√5 + √3)² = (√5)² + 2√5√3 + (√3)² = 8 + 2√15."
          ],
          "note": "In Example 1(c) the printed book is missing a plus sign: \"(√2 × 1)(√2 × √5)\" should read (√2 × 1) + (√2 × √5)."
        },
